    "problem": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "paper",
        "layout": "band",
        "numbered": true,
        "eyebrow": "The problem",
        "heading": "Four ways a walk gets your feet wet",
        "lede": "<p>Only one of these is the thing boots are designed to stop. That’s why good boots and wet feet aren’t a contradiction.</p>",
        "mirror": false
      },
      "blocks": {
        "i1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Over the top",
            "body": "<p>Wet grass, bracken and heather put water onto the boot from above, where the tongue and the laces are. Gaiters help. They don’t seal.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "The boot soaks through",
            "body": "<p>The outer fabric saturates, the boot stops breathing, and everything your foot produces stays inside. It isn’t leaking — it’s full. This is the four-hour problem.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Streams and bog",
            "body": "<p>There’s a depth beyond which no footwear helps, and on wet British ground you meet it more often than the forecast suggests.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i4": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "From the inside",
            "body": "<p>Eight hours of walking produces a lot of moisture. Plenty of people with perfectly good boots finish the day damp anyway.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "i1",
        "i2",
        "i3",
        "i4"
      ]
    },
    "answers": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "ink",
        "layout": "boxes",
        "numbered": true,
        "eyebrow": "The answer",
        "heading": "What the socks do about it",
        "lede": "<p>The waterproof layer sits inside the sock rather than inside the boot. That changes where the waterproof line is, and what happens when the boot gives up.</p>",
        "link_label": "How the three layers work",
        "link_url": "/pages/technology",
        "mirror": false
      },
      "blocks": {
        "i1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Your boot can be soaked and your foot dry",
            "body": "<p>Once the waterproof layer is on your foot, a wet boot is uncomfortable rather than a disaster. You stop planning the route around the puddles.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "They work with any boot, including a leaky one",
            "body": "<p>Most people find waterproof socks because their boots have started letting water in. It’s the cheapest useful thing to try before spending two hundred pounds.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "They aren’t a replacement for boots",
            "body": "<p>Said plainly here as well as on the homepage. If your footwear is wrong for the ground, this won’t fix it. It solves wet, not grip or ankle support.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "i1",
